WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.420
(pleasant orchestral music)

00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:03.320
- [Narrator] This is the 2018

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:05.830
Victoria's Secret Fashion Show.

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:08.320
The hour-long nationally-televised event

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:09.710
has served as a showcase

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:12.893
for the brand's bras
and lingerie since 2001,

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:17.790
but 2018 was the fashion
show's last broadcast.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:20.900
The show had suffered several
years of declining ratings

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:23.920
that coincided with the
drastic rise then fall

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:24.810
of the stock price

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:27.883
of Victoria's Secret
parent company, L Brands.

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:31.910
Here's how the lingerie
retailer, Angels and all,

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:34.330
rose to the top of women's fashion

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:35.660
and how its fall from grace

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:38.483
has created an uncertain
future for the company.

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:43.830
In 1982, L Brands owner Leslie Wexner

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:45.780
acquired Victoria's Secret,

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.120
a small retailer consisting of six stores

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.303
and a catalog for $1 million.

00:00:51.325 --> 00:00:54.140
Wexner developed many
successful retail businesses

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:55.363
in the 1980s and '90s.

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:59.080
- He popularized the
specialty mall retailer,

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:01.180
which had tightly-focused product lines

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:02.830
and carefully-designed stores,

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:04.610
and he took those stores
and rolled them out

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:06.210
to malls across the country.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:09.280
That model really upended
the department store model.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:10.113
- [Narrator] In the mid 1990s,

00:01:10.113 --> 00:01:13.010
Victoria's Secret's
heavily-padded Miracle Bra,

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:15.900
designed to create cleavage,
was vying for market share

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.320
with its competitor, the WonderBra.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:21.460
- [Announcer] Only Victoria's
Secret has the Miracle Bra.

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:23.150
- Get a Miracle Bra.

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:25.360
- [Narrator] Victoria's
Secret doubled down on ads

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:27.040
with provocative TV commercials

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:30.463
and launched its first
fashion show in 1995.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:33.610
- Victoria's Secret tried
to define what sexy was

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:35.460
and what a sexy woman should look like,

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:37.110
and you can really see by the ads

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:39.920
that they were pushing a
very specific beauty ideal.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:41.380
That image was executed

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:44.630
by L Brands Chief Marketing
Officer Edward Razek,

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:45.760
who was put in charge

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.750
of casting models for the fashion show.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:49.870
- [Narrator] Throughout the 2000s,

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:52.460
Leslie Wexner sold many
of his apparel brands

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:56.110
and focused more and more on
his lingerie and beauty lines.

00:01:56.110 --> 00:01:59.020
This included launching PINK in 2004,

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:01.840
a Victoria's Secret spinoff for teens.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:03.830
Unlike many other retailers,

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:05.830
Victoria's Secret continued to grow

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:08.040
after the 2008 recession.

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:11.730
The retailer opened more
stores and sales expanded,

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:16.520
and L Brands stock price rose until 2016.

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:18.160
- That's when you really
started to see trouble

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:21.470
for the company as consumer
tastes started to shift.

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:23.410
- [Narrator] Athleisure
began to dominate fashion

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:24.880
and growth began to slow

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:27.350
in Victoria's Secret's bra business.

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:30.990
This was driven in part by
consumers preferring bralettes,

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:33.650
bras with no underwire or padding.

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:36.010
- There was a shift in what
female consumers wanted

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:38.510
from their underwear and what
they were willing to pay.

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:41.500
Many younger women didn't
wanna pay $60 anymore

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:44.040
for a padded bra that
they saw on a supermodel.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.590
Many of them preferred stuff
like more authentic marketing,

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:49.520
natural looks, and bras built for comfort.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:52.800
- [Narrator] In 2016, Leslie
Wexner assumed direct control

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.050
of Victoria's Secret.

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:56.890
He eliminated swimwear,
stopped mailing out catalogs,

00:02:56.890 --> 00:02:59.520
and doubled down on sports bras.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:04.110
By 2018, L Brands share
price had fallen 50%,

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:08.260
erasing about $14 billion
in investor's wealth.

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:10.890
Victoria's Secret started
to appear out of touch

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.860
with female consumers
around the same time.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.840
- A major stumbling block
for Victoria's Secret

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:17.440
was when Ed Razek told Vogue

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:19.050
that he didn't think transgender models

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:20.720
should be cast in the fashion show.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:23.130
He was forced to apologize
for those remarks,

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:26.220
and a lot of people called for
his resignation immediately.

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:28.370
He actually stayed on a while after that

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:30.623
and eventually resigned in 2019.

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:34.050
Victoria's Secret's internal
culture and management

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:36.320
has come under scrutiny as well.

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:39.010
- Ed Razek, after he
left Victoria's Secret,

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:42.550
has faced allegations of
harassing models and employees,

00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:44.670
and Les Wexner has come under fire

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:46.430
for his ties to Jeffrey Epstein,

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:48.780
who was his longtime money manager.

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:51.040
- [Narrator] Leslie Wexner
has said that he cut ties

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:53.420
with Epstein more than a decade ago

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:57.270
after Epstein was charged
with child sex trafficking.

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:59.970
Independent directors
on the L Brands Board

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:02.590
responded to the sexual
harassment allegations

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:05.220
in a statement that said
the company had instituted

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:08.810
robust anti-harassment
policies in recent years

00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:11.010
and the company regrets any instance

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:13.250
where we did not achieve this objective

00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:16.623
of a safe, welcoming,
and empowering workplace.

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:19.720
Razek told the New York
Times that the allegations

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:22.197
were, quote, "categorically untrue,

00:04:22.197 --> 00:04:25.610
"misconstrued, or taking out of context."

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:27.630
- Consumers have a very particular idea

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:29.320
of what Victoria's Secret is.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:31.270
It'll be interesting
to see whether a brand

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:33.880
that's married to such a
particular type of marketing

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:36.202
can transform itself into anything else.

00:04:36.202 --> 00:04:39.869
(pleasant orchestral music)

